# tests/rxtsource/fixtures/bench_altwide_0_2.rxtin
#
# THE DOGFOOD ([DD-13b.W1.3], part 2): a pcrec-bench SUB-BENCH's pattern set
# as a `.rxt` SOURCE, verbatim.
#
# PROVENANCE
#   source repo   /home/duxevents/pcrec-bench (the sibling benchmark project)
#   set           bench/altwide, `subbench.toml` version "0.2"
#   patterns      bench/altwide/patterns/*.rx, 33 files, at that repo's
#                 commit 0ef1ee9 (2026-09-02)
#   copied        2026-09-03, BY HAND, for [DD-13b.W1.3]
#   replaced by   the bench's own `.rxt` exporter, once the name rules below
#                 are accepted (their O-13 ask; our answer is the manager's
#                 syntax ruling, implemented in this step)
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR, and it is not a test of the patterns. Nothing here asserts
# what any of them MATCH; the bench owns those expectations and it owns the
# oracle that produced them. This file exists so the FORMAT has a real
# consumer's real set in it — 33 pattern ids a person chose, of which 32
# carry a `-` and could not be block names before this step, and one whole
# alternation of 4,096 branches that is a genuinely hostile input for a
# single `pattern` line. A format exercised only by fixtures its own author
# wrote is a format tested against its author's alphabet (D27's argument, one
# tier up).
#
# WHAT IT PROVES ABOUT LOSSLESSNESS. MEASURED 2026-09-03 over all 33: no
# newline, no tab, no byte outside 0x20-0x7e, and no leading or trailing
# whitespace — so `pattern <text>` carries each one VERBATIM with no escape
# and no transformation, and the `.rxt` round trip is the identity. The
# bench's O-13 section 4(a) claimed exactly this for the 77 patterns it had
# then; re-measured here across all four sets, the count is now 90 and the
# claim still holds for every one of them.
#
# WHAT IT DECLARES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. `name` and `target`
# and nothing else: no `config`, no `flags`, no `engine`, no `budget`, no
# `encoding`. That is the bench's own condition (O-13 section 4(b)) and it is
# right — D93 makes a source's composed config WIN over a command-line flag,
# so a set file carrying an `engine` line would pin the testee matrix from
# inside the set, which is the harness's decision and not the set's.
#
# THE NAMES. Every block name here is the bench's `pattern_id` unchanged, and
# 32 of the 33 are not C identifiers. `target = <name>` derives the symbol
# prefix by mapping `-` and `.` to `_`, so `w-8` builds as `w_8`. Two names
# mapping to one prefix would be refused naming both; MEASURED across the
# bench's 90 ids the mapping collides exactly once, on `floor`, and that
# collision is CROSS-SET (each of the four sets has its own `floor.rx`), so
# no single-set export collides and the refusal fires exactly where sets
# would be merged.
#
# ITS `.rxtin` EXTENSION IS LOAD-BEARING: `tests/harness/run.sh` finds
# `*.rxt`, so a `.rxt` here would join the corpus and be compiled and scored
# as one of ours. This file is an INPUT to `tests/rxtsource/`, which copies
# it to a `.rxt` in a scratch directory when it wants one.


target = ci-256
target = ci-512
target = cnt-64
target = floor
target = nar4-256
target = nar4-512
target = nar4-64
target = pfx3-256
target = pfx3-512
target = s-2048
target = s-256
target = s-4096
target = s-512
target = sfx-256
target = sfx-512
target = sfx-64
target = sh1-256
target = sh1-512
target = sh1-64
target = srt-256
target = srt-512
target = w-1024
target = w-128
target = w-192
target = w-2048
target = w-256
target = w-384
target = w-512
target = w-64
target = w-8
target = w-96
target = wb-256
target = wb-512

